\section{Numerical evidence}\label{sec:numerics}

This section compares \cref{thm:main} with exact computations of $V_f(x,y)$. Everything here is evidence and plays no part in the proofs. All tables and plots are regenerated by \nolinkurl{manuscript/reproduce.py} (see \nolinkurl{manuscript/README.md}). That script re-runs the accepted computation from its source code and recompiles its C\texttt{++} part; it also computes the theoretical quantities.

\subsection{What was computed}\label{sec:num-method}

\emph{Grid.} The three polynomials are $f(k)=k^2+1$, $k^3+2$ and $2k+1$, with weights $f'(k)=2k$, $3k^2$ and $2$. All three satisfy~\eqref{eq:f-hyp}. The values of $x$ are $x=2.5\cdot10^5\cdot2^j$ for $0\le j\le10$, so the largest is $x=2.56\cdot10^8$. For each $x$ and each target ratio $r\in\{0.01,0.02,0.05,0.1,0.2,0.4,0.7,1\}$, $y$ is the largest integer with $f(y)\le rx$. At the two largest $x$ the ratio $r=0.005$ was added as a diagnostic. The quantity reported is the normalised variance
\[
N(x,r)=\frac{V_f(x,y)}{x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2}},
\]
computed with the actual ratio $f(y)/x$. By \cref{thm:main}(iii), $N(x,r)\to C_f$ when $x\to\infty$ and $f(y)/x\to0$ suitably.

\emph{Method.} The computation (archived numerical snapshot, files \texttt{compute.py} and \texttt{counts.cpp}) is exact in all integer quantities.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item The squarefree indicator $\mu^2(n)$, $n\le x$, is sieved. All autocorrelations
\[
C(h)=\sum_{1\le n\le x-h}\mu^2(n)\mu^2(n+h),\qquad0\le h\le x,
\]
are obtained from one number-theoretic transform modulo the prime $p_0=3221225473$, with primitive root~$5$. Every $C(h)$ is at most $x<p_0$, so no information is lost.
\item As in the proof of \cref{prop:identity}, $\sum_lQ(x;q,l)^2=Q(x)+2\sum_{m\ge1}C(mq)$ for every modulus $q=f(k)$.
\item The densities are $g(q,l)=\frac6{\pi^2}\tilde g(q,l)$ with an explicit rational $\tilde g(q,l)$ equivalent to \cref{prop:densities}(i),(iii). The coefficients $\sum_lQ(x;q,l)\tilde g(q,l)$ and $\sum_l\tilde g(q,l)^2$ are computed as exact rationals, using the counts of squarefree multiples of $d$ as in the proof of \cref{prop:trivial}.
\item Finally $V(x;q)=\sum_lQ^2-2x\sum_lgQ+x^2\sum_lg^2$, as in~\eqref{eq:id1}, is accumulated with the weights $f'(k)$ in $70$-digit decimal arithmetic.
\end{enumerate}
The snapshot validates the implementation in several ways. It checks the transform against direct lag sums at every $x$. It compares $\tilde g$ with brute-force local densities for all $q\le50$ and with empirical frequencies at $x=10^6$. At $x=257$ it compares every generated modulus with direct residue counts. During the original run, the operator and an integrator also recomputed some rows independently by direct counting (Appendix~\ref{app:provenance}).

\emph{Re-run.} For this write-up the snapshot code was run again, unchanged, after its SHA-256 hashes had been checked against the acceptance record. The C\texttt{++} part was compiled locally with \texttt{clang++ -O3}. All $270$ rows ($90$ per polynomial, including the diagnostic rows) reproduce the archived integer counts exactly. The values of $V_f$ agree to all stored digits (largest relative difference $\RerunMaxRelV$), and all fitted quantities are identical. The full grid needs about $5$\,GB of memory; the running times of the re-run that produced the tables are recorded in \texttt{reproduction.json} (fields \texttt{snapshot\_rerun.seconds} and \texttt{snapshot\_rerun.total\_seconds\_of\_reproduce\_py}).

\subsection{The constants \texorpdfstring{$C_f$}{C\_f}}\label{sec:num-constants}

For the three polynomials the root counts are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k^2+1$: $\rho(2)=1$, $\rho(4)=0$, and $\rho(p)=\rho(p^2)=1+\bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\bigr)$ for odd $p$;
\item $k^3+2$: $\rho(2)=\rho(3)=1$, $\rho(4)=\rho(9)=0$; for $p\ge5$, $\rho(p^2)=\rho(p)$, with $\rho(p)=1$ if $p\equiv2\pmod 3$, and, if $p\equiv1\pmod3$, $\rho(p)=3$ or $0$ according as $(-2)^{(p-1)/3}\equiv1\pmod p$ or not;
\item $2k+1$: $\rho(2)=\rho(4)=0$, and $\rho(p)=\rho(p^2)=1$ for odd $p$.
\end{itemize}
For primes not dividing the discriminant all roots are simple, and each root modulo $p$ lifts to exactly one root modulo $p^2$, which gives $\rho(p^2)=\rho(p)$. The formulas were checked by brute force modulo $p$ and $p^2$ for all $p<\RhoBound$. None of the three polynomials has a content prime, so $P_0=\emptyset$, and~\eqref{eq:sigma-close} holds for every $p$ with $K=3+2d$. Hence, for $N\ge30$,
\[
\Bigl|\log\prod_{p>N}\sigma_p\Bigr|\le\frac{K}{1-K/N^2}\sum_{p>N}\frac1{p^2}\le\frac{8K}{1-K/N^2}\Bigl(\frac1{N^2}+\frac1{30N}\Bigr).
\]
Here we used that every prime $p>N\ge30$ lies in one of the $8$ reduced residue classes modulo $30$, and that each class contributes at most $N^{-2}+(30N)^{-1}$. \Cref{tab:constants} lists the partial values $\frac23I\prod_{p\le N}\sigma_p$ and the resulting interval for $C_f$ with $N=10^7$. The partial values are rounded to nine decimals; the interval endpoints are rounded outwards, so that each printed interval contains the computed one. The intervals account rigorously for the omitted primes. They do not account for floating-point rounding in the partial products, which are computed in double precision. As a check of that rounding (not a certified bound), the partial products for $N=10^5$ were recomputed in $40$-digit arithmetic; they agree with the double-precision values to a relative difference of at most $\FloatCheck$.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\input{generated/tab_constants}
\caption{Partial Euler products $\frac23I\prod_{p\le N}\sigma_p$ and intervals for $C_f$. The row ``$t$ (formal)'' uses $\rho\equiv1$. The last row is Vaughan's constant~\eqref{eq:vaughan-c2} with $C_1$ truncated at $p\le N$; its limit equals that of the row above (\cref{rem:vaughan}).}
\label{tab:constants}
\end{table}

Apart from floating-point rounding, the intervals therefore fix the digits
\[
C_{k^2+1}=\CfQuadCert\ldots,\qquad C_{k^3+2}=\CfCubicCert\ldots,\qquad C_{2k+1}=\CfLinCert\ldots .
\]
The partial products at $N=10^7$ give the approximate values $C_{k^2+1}\approx\CfQuad$, $C_{k^3+2}\approx\CfCubic$ and $C_{2k+1}\approx\CfLin$; the digits beyond those fixed above are only indicative. For $f(t)=t$ the formal value $\frac23I\prod_{p\le 10^7}\sigma_p\approx\CfT$ agrees with Vaughan's $c_2\approx\VaughanCtwo$ (with $C_1$ truncated at the same point), as \cref{rem:vaughan} predicts. The local identity behind that remark, the identities of \cref{lem:local}, and $\zeta(-\frac12)=-\zeta(\frac32)/(4\pi)$ were also verified with computer algebra and $80$-digit arithmetic (\texttt{reproduce.py}, function \texttt{symbolic\_local\_identity}).

\subsection{The data and the predicted constant}\label{sec:num-data}

\Cref{tab:largest} shows $N(x,r)$ at the largest $x$, together with the relative change from $x=1.28\cdot10^8$. The full tables are in Appendix~\ref{app:tables}. Two features are visible.

First, at fixed $r$ the normalised values are stable in $x$. Between the two largest sizes they change by at most $\StabCubic\%$ for $k^3+2$, and by at most $\StabOther\%$ for $k^2+1$ and $2k+1$ (see also \cref{fig:x}). Fits of $\log V_f$ against $\log x$ at fixed target ratio give $\alpha+\beta\approx2$ (\cref{tab:fits}), consistent with the homogeneous main term $x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$.

Second, $N(x,r)$ depends clearly on $r$. It rises from values between $\NDiagMin$ and $\NDiagMax$ at $r=0.005$ to values between $\NOneMin$ and $\NOneMax$ at $r=1$. A fit of $\log V_f$ against $\log f(y)$ at fixed $x$ therefore gives exponents $\beta$ between $\BetaMin$ and $\BetaMax$ instead of $\frac32$ (\cref{tab:fits}). This does not contradict \cref{thm:main}. Part (iii) concerns $f(y)/x\to0$, and at a fixed ratio $r$ the error term $x^{1/3+\eps}Y^{5/3}$ of part (i) is as large as the main term. The ratio dependence is captured by the exact-shape statement~(ii); see \cref{sec:num-shape}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\input{generated/tab_largest}
\caption{$N(x,r)=V_f(x,y)/(x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2})$ at $x=2.56\cdot10^8$ for the target ratios $r$ (the column $0.005$ is the diagnostic ratio). The ``change'' rows give the relative change, in percent, from $x=1.28\cdot10^8$ to $x=2.56\cdot10^8$.}
\label{tab:largest}
\end{table}

\emph{Extrapolation to $r\to0$.} The snapshot estimates $\lim_{r\to0}N$ empirically. At the largest $x$ it fits the model $N(r)=C+a\sqrt r+br$ by unweighted least squares to the rows with $r$ at or above a ``stable'' cutoff. The cutoff is the smallest requested ratio such that every row at or above it changes by less than $1\%$ between the two largest $x$; here it is $\StableCutoff$ for all three polynomials. The reported uncertainty is a \emph{heuristic sensitivity envelope}: the largest of twice the formal standard error of the intercept, the spread over neighbouring cutoffs, the shift to the previous $x$, the shifts under the alternative models $C+a\sqrt r$ and $C+a\sqrt r+br+cr^{3/2}$, and the shifts when the fitted window is cut at $r\le0.1$, $0.2$ or $0.4$. It is not a confidence interval and not an error bound, and the model $C+a\sqrt r+br$ itself is not derived from the theorem. \Cref{tab:fits} compares the extrapolated constants with $C_f$. The relative differences are $\RelQuad\%$, $\RelCubic\%$ and $\RelLin\%$ for $k^2+1$, $k^3+2$ and $2k+1$, all within the envelopes. \Cref{tab:sensitivity} shows how strongly the extrapolated value depends on the model. For $k^2+1$ the alternatives range from about $\SensQuadMin$ to $\SensQuadMax$ and bracket $C_f$, so the difference of $\RelQuad\%$ reflects the choice of model rather than the constant. \Cref{fig:ratio} shows the data, the fitted curves and $C_f$.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\input{generated/tab_fits}
\caption{Empirical exponents and extrapolated constants from the snapshot fits, compared with $C_f$ from \cref{tab:constants}. ``$\alpha+\beta$'' is the mean of the eight fixed-ratio slopes of $\log V_f$ against $\log x$ (with the across-ratio standard error); ``$\beta$'' is the fixed-$x$ slope against $\log f(y)$ at the largest $x$ (with its regression standard error). The last column is $(C_{\mathrm{extr}}-C_f)$ divided by the heuristic envelope.}
\label{tab:fits}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\footnotesize
\input{generated/tab_sensitivity}
\caption{Sensitivity of the extrapolated constant: the primary fit, the two alternative models, the window $r\le0.1$, the lower cutoff $0.005$, and the primary fit at the previous size $x=1.28\cdot10^8$.}
\label{tab:sensitivity}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=0.82\textwidth,height=0.5\textwidth,xlabel={$\sqrt{r}$, with $r=f(y)/x$},ylabel={$N(x,r)$},xmin=-0.02,xmax=1.02,ymin=0.13,ymax=0.19,
  legend pos=north west,legend cell align=left,legend style={font=\footnotesize},tick label style={font=\footnotesize},
  yticklabel style={/pgf/number format/fixed,/pgf/number format/precision=3}]
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.6pt,blue] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_ratio_0.dat};
\addlegendentry{$k^2+1$}
\addplot[only marks,mark=square*,mark size=1.6pt,red] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_ratio_1.dat};
\addlegendentry{$k^3+2$}
\addplot[only marks,mark=triangle*,mark size=2pt,black!60!green] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_ratio_2.dat};
\addlegendentry{$2k+1$}
\addplot[blue,dashed,forget plot] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_fit_0.dat};
\addplot[red,dashed,forget plot] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_fit_1.dat};
\addplot[black!60!green,dashed,forget plot] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_fit_2.dat};
\addplot[only marks,mark=o,mark size=3pt,blue,thick,forget plot] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_cf_0.dat};
\addplot[only marks,mark=o,mark size=3pt,red,thick,forget plot] table[x=sqrtr,y=N]{generated/fig_cf_1.dat};
